<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <PropertyGroup>
    <RootNamespace>pwiz.Osprey.IO</RootNamespace>
    <AssemblyName>Osprey.IO</AssemblyName>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <InternalsVisibleTo Include="Osprey.Test" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <!-- ReSharper disable once VulnerablePackage -->
    <PackageReference Include="Parquet.Net" Version="4.25.0" />
    <!-- Snappier arrives transitively (Parquet.Net -> IronCompress -> Snappier).
         Pinned to 1.3.1 because the range resolves to 1.1.6, which carries
         GHSA-pggp-6c3x-2xmx; 1.3.1 is the first clean release. Remove once
         Parquet.Net is on 6.x, which depends on [1.3.1,) itself. -->
    <PackageReference Include="Snappier" Version="1.3.1" />
    <!-- 1.0.119 to match pwiz-sharp, whose Bruker and UIMF readers pull the same
         package (a lower version here is an NU1605 downgrade error). -->
    <PackageReference Include="System.Data.SQLite.Core" Version="1.0.119" />
    <PackageReference Include="Newtonsoft.Json" Version="13.0.3" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="..\Osprey.Core\Osprey.Core.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!-- ProteoWizard, as the ONLY spectrum reader (issue #4497), reached through the
       same wrapper Skyline uses rather than through pwiz-sharp directly. The wrapper
       was net8.0-windows until CommonUtil's WinForms half moved to CommonBaseUI; it is
       plain net10.0 now, so a plain net10.0 Osprey can reference it and still run on
       Linux without the Wine container.

       ONE reference in place of twelve, and more to the point one definition of the read
       semantics - MsDataFileImpl carries the ReaderConfig, the vendor centroiding and the
       vendor reader registration that SpectrumFileReader.Vendors.cs used to duplicate
       here. It pulls the same eight vendor projects transitively; a file Skyline can open
       is a file Osprey can open, by construction rather than by matching lists.

       The vendor projects still carry no SDK unless the build opts in to the licenses
       (pwiz-sharp's IAgreeToVendorLicenses, plus NativeVendorsAvailable for the
       Windows-only native SDKs); without it they compile in NO_VENDOR_SUPPORT mode where
       Identify() works and Read() throws. So this reference costs a default developer
       clone nothing.

       KNOWN ISSUE, unchanged by this swap and deliberately not papered over: a Release
       Osprey builds and deploys the DEBUG build of the referenced assemblies. Building a
       .sln unsets Configuration and Platform across a ProjectReference to a project that
       is not in that solution (MSBuild's ShouldUnsetParentConfigurationAndPlatform, on by
       default), and neither ProteowizardWrapper nor pwiz-sharp is in Osprey.sln. The code
       is correct either way - every gate and every byte comparison for #4497 was run in
       this state - so the cost is an unoptimized ProteoWizard doing the spectrum reading,
       not a wrong answer.

       SetConfiguration/SetPlatform metadata and clearing the property were both tried and
       BOTH MADE IT WORSE: Osprey.exe and Osprey.Test re-resolve these references
       transitively, each hop negotiating its own property set, so a clean Release build
       then produced pwiz-sharp under bin/Release, bin/Debug AND bin/x64 and deployed an
       assembly matching none of them. The fix is to give these projects ONE owner - put
       them in Osprey.sln, the way Skyline carries its shared projects - which is a
       structural change worth its own commit rather than a rider on the reader swap. -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="..\..\Shared\ProteowizardWrapper\ProteowizardWrapper.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!-- PROdotnetzip (Ionic.Zlib) for stock-zlib-compatible deflate output.
       Maintained fork of the deprecated DotNetZip; same Ionic namespaces and
       verified byte-identical deflate, so the parity below is unaffected.
       Matches Skyline's BlibData writer (Util/Extensions/UtilDB.Compress)
       so Osprey .blib peak blobs are byte-identical to Skyline's
       and to Rust osprey's flate2 output. .NET's built-in DeflateStream
       produces a 4-byte-shorter non-stock-zlib variant which would split
       cross-impl byte parity. -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageReference Include="PROdotnetzip" Version="1.20.0" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
